    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Imaginary-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the sine-valued time signal obtained by projecting a rotating phasor onto the imaginary axis and interpreting the result as its imaginary component.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Imaginary-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Imaginary-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "image-needed",
        "image_slots": [
          {
            "image_slot_id": "section-001",
            "placement": "section",
            "location_placeholder": "[IMAGE-LOCATION:section-001]",
            "description_placeholder": "[IMAGE-DESCRIPTION:section-001]",
            "image_description": "A clean instructional complex-plane diagram with horizontal and vertical axes, a phasor vector of magnitude $A$ starting at the origin and ending at angle $\\theta(t)$ from the positive real axis, a dashed vertical projection down to the imaginary axis, the projection labeled $A\\sin\\theta(t)$, and a small curved arrow indicating counterclockwise rotation. Keep the diagram uncluttered, mathematical, and precise, with standard axis labels and no decorative elements."
          }
        ],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the phasor picture, the tip moves in the complex plane. The imaginary-axis shadow is the sine-valued signal we want, because that shadow is the phasor's imaginary component.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "image",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": "section-001"
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with a unit phasor on the positive real axis, then rotate it to angle $\\theta$. Its imaginary-axis projection is the vertical component, so a phasor of magnitude $A$ at angle $\\theta$ projects to $A\\sin\\theta$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{Im}\\{A e^{j\\theta}\\}=A\\sin\\theta",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In complex-number language, that rule is just taking the imaginary part of $A e^{j\\theta}$. Keep this notation fixed; it is the same quantity the picture is showing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now let the angle depend on time as $\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi$ for counterclockwise rotation. The same projection becomes the time-domain signal we read from the imaginary axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi,\\qquad x(t)=\\operatorname{Im}\\{A e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)}\\}=A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the bridge is simple: imaginary-axis projection equals imaginary part, and a rotating phasor produces $x(t)=A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$. Later sections only vary magnitude, phase, and rotation direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Imaginary-Axis Projection of a Phasor",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Projecting a Unit Counterclockwise Phasor onto the Imaginary Axis",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A unit phasor starts on the positive real axis and rotates counterclockwise with angular frequency $\\omega$. Write its imaginary-axis projection signal $x(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the simplest case: the phasor has unit magnitude, no initial phase offset, and counterclockwise rotation. That means the angle grows as $\\theta(t)=\\omega t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\omega t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an imaginary-axis projection, take the vertical component of the rotating phasor. In complex form, that is the imaginary part of $e^{j\\theta(t)}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\operatorname{Im}\\{e^{j\\theta(t)}\\}=\\operatorname{Im}\\{e^{j\\omega t}\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use Euler's form to separate the real and imaginary parts: $e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)$. The imaginary part is the sine term, so the projected signal is $x(t)=\\sin(\\omega t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the unit counterclockwise phasor maps directly to the sine-valued time signal $x(t)=\\sin(\\omega t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
